{"annotationScore": 3.0, "comments": [{"commentType": "FUNCTION", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000303", "id": "17576170", "source": "PubMed"}, {"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000303", "id": "20176268", "source": "PubMed"}, {"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000303", "id": "22158414", "source": "PubMed"}, {"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000303", "id": "24600447", "source": "PubMed"}], "value": "V region of the variable domain of immunoglobulin heavy chains that participates in the antigen recognition (PubMed:24600447). Immunoglobulins, also known as antibodies, are membrane-bound or secreted glycoproteins produced by B lymphocytes. In the recognition phase of humoral immunity, the membrane-bound immunoglobulins serve as receptors which, upon binding of a specific antigen, trigger the clonal expansion and differentiation of B lymphocytes into immunoglobulins-secreting plasma cells. Secreted immunoglobulins mediate the effector phase of humoral immunity, which results in the elimination of bound antigens (PubMed:20176268, PubMed:22158414). The antigen binding site is formed by the variable domain of one heavy chain, together with that of its associated light chain. Thus, each immunoglobulin has two antigen binding sites with remarkable affinity for a particular antigen. The variable domains are assembled by a process called V-(D)-J rearrangement and can then be subjected to somatic hypermutations which, after exposure to antigen and selection, allow affinity maturation for a particular antigen (PubMed:17576170, PubMed:20176268)"}]}, {"commentType": "SUBUNIT", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000303", "id": "20176268", "source": "PubMed"}], "value": "Immunoglobulins are composed of two identical heavy chains and two identical light chains; disulfide-linked"}]}, {"commentType": "SUBCELLULAR LOCATION", "subcellularLocations": [{"location": {"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000303", "id": "20176268", "source": "PubMed"}, {"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000303", "id": "22158414", "source": "PubMed"}], "id": "SL-0243", "value": "Secreted"}}, {"location": {"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000303", "id": "20176268", "source": "PubMed"}, {"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000303", "id": "22158414", "source": "PubMed"}], "id": "SL-0039", "value": "Cell membrane"}}]}, {"commentType": "POLYMORPHISM", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000305"}], "value": "There are several alleles. The sequence shown is that of IMGT allele IGHV3-30-5*01"}]}, {"commentType": "CAUTION", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000305"}], "value": "For examples of full-length immunoglobulin heavy chains (of different isotypes) see AC P0DOX2, AC P0DOX3, AC P0DOX4, AC P0DOX5 and AC P0DOX6"}]}, {"commentType": "CAUTION", "texts": [{"evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000305", "id": "23541343", "source": "PubMed"}], "value": "Product of a polymorphic gene. This sequence cannot be differentiated from this of the IGHV3-30 gene. Watson et al. identified this gene on chromosome 14. However, it is not currently present on the reference genome assembly (GRCh38/hg38)"}]}], "entryAudit": {"entryVersion": 42, "firstPublicDate": "2017-04-12", "lastAnnotationUpdateDate": "2026-11-04", "lastSequenceUpdateDate": "2017-04-12", "sequenceVersion": 1}, "entryType": "UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)", "extraAttributes": {"countByCommentType": {"CAUTION": 2, "FUNCTION": 1, "POLYMORPHISM": 1, "SUBCELLULAR LOCATION": 1, "SUBUNIT": 1}, "countByFeatureType": {"Chain": 1, "Disulfide bond": 1, "Domain": 1, "Modified residue": 1, "Non-terminal residue": 1, "Region": 6, "Signal": 1}, "uniParcId": "UPI0002B62496"}, "features": [{"description": "", "evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000255"}], "location": {"end": {"modifier": "EXACT", "value": 19}, "start": {"modifier": "EXACT", "value": 1}}, "type": "Signal"}, {"description": "Immunoglobulin heavy variable 3-30-5", "evidences": [{"evidenceCode": "ECO:0000255"}], "featureId": "PRO_0000439564", "location": {"end": {"modifier": "EXA